tell you that people who are industrious

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:32.100
come up with a strategy for solving the
problem that's ahead of them and then

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:35.790
they do whatever they can to stick to
the strategy and so for example if you

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:42.000
sat down today or tomorrow for a couple
of hours three hours and you filled in a

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:47.549
google calendar whatever you happen to
use with a strategy for studying and a

00:21:47.549 --> 00:21:51.480
list of when all your assignments are
due and all of that and when you're

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:55.650
going to sit down and study then you
won't be in a position where you have to

00:21:55.650 --> 00:22:01.380
cram for 10 hours a day hopelessly right
before you know an important exam it's

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:05.549
also a very ineffective way of studying
by the way I mean first of all people

00:22:05.549 --> 00:22:08.730
who cram for 10 hours say they're
studying for 10 hours but they rarely

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:15.030
are because well I can't study for 10
hours I don't have the power of

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:18.780
concentration that would enable me to do
that for that prolonged period of time

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:22.860
I can manage about three hours of
intense intellectual activity before I'm

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:27.780
pretty done and it's also the case that
if you study and then sleep and then

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:30.119
study and then sleep and then study and
then sleep

00:22:30.119 --> 00:22:34.979
you space it out then you're much more
likely to remember it's also much more

00:22:34.979 --> 00:22:38.369
likely that you're you're much more
likely to remember if you try to recall

00:22:38.369 --> 00:22:42.509
the material and so highlighting and
that sort of thing isn't very useful but

00:22:42.509 --> 00:22:47.189
reading closing the book summarizing
what you've read without opening the

00:22:47.189 --> 00:22:51.589
damn book that's useful and the reason
for that is that you're practicing

00:22:51.589 --> 00:22:55.759
remembering and that's what you have to
practice if you're practicing

00:22:55.759 --> 00:22:59.969
memorization you have to practice
remembering you don't just go over the

00:22:59.969 --> 00:23:02.999
thing over and over
that'll help you with recognition memory

00:23:02.999 --> 00:23:08.159
but some but it won't help you with
recall memory so anyways the last trade

00:23:08.159 --> 00:23:12.929
is openness openness is a creativity
trait it's also associated with

00:23:12.929 --> 00:23:17.429
intelligence in that intelligent people
and I'm speaking technically of IQ tend

00:23:17.429 --> 00:23:22.369
to be higher and tend to be more
creative which is hardly surprising

00:23:22.369 --> 00:23:28.159
creative people are more likely to be
liberal politically by the way they like

00:23:28.159 --> 00:23:33.539
novelty they like aesthetics they like
fiction they like movies they like art

00:23:33.539 --> 00:23:38.239
they like poetry there is something
about them that grants them an aesthetic

00:23:38.239 --> 00:23:45.389
sensitivity and and that's a that's an
inbuilt trait and it's not the case by

00:23:45.389 --> 00:23:49.409
the way that everyone's creative in fact
far from it we've used the creative

00:23:49.409 --> 00:23:53.939
achievement questionnaire to to measure
people's creativity I'll talk to you

00:23:53.939 --> 00:23:58.289
about that later in the class and the
creative achievement questionnaire takes

00:23:58.289 --> 00:24:04.409
13 dimensions of creativity so you know
writing dancing acting scientific

00:24:04.409 --> 00:24:11.369
investigation entrepreneurial activity
architectural activity cooking there's a

00:24:11.369 --> 00:24:15.359
there's a handful of others singing etc
you know the sorts of things that you

00:24:15.359 --> 00:24:19.619
would assume that people could be
creative about and then it asks people

00:24:19.619 --> 00:24:24.619
to rate themselves on a scale from one
to ten on their level of achievement

00:24:24.619 --> 00:24:28.909
with regards to all those creative
domains with zero being I have no

00:24:28.909 --> 00:24:35.279
training or proficiency in this area and
70% of people score zero across the

00:24:35.279 --> 00:24:38.639
entire creative achievement
questionnaire a tiny proportion of

00:24:38.639 --> 00:24:42.419
people are outliers way out and they're
creative in many dimensions

00:24:42.419 --> 00:24:46.350
simultaneously and it
exceptionally creative and it turns out

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:49.650
as you'll find out that that pattern
which is called a Pareto distribution

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:54.630
where most people stack up at zero and a
few people are way out on the creative

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:58.890
end characterizes all sorts of
distributions like the distribution of

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:05.640
money for example which is why 1% of the
people have the overwhelming majority of

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:11.669
the money it's a different 1% across
time it like it churns and you're much

00:25:11.669 --> 00:25:16.559
more likely to be in the 1% if you're
older logically enough because one of

00:25:16.559 --> 00:25:21.390
the things you do as you age is you
trade youth for money if you're

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:25.289
fortunate I don't think the trade is
really worth it but that's the best

00:25:25.289 --> 00:25:31.350
you've got so anyways those particular
traits you can think of those as ways

00:25:31.350 --> 00:25:35.669
that you simplify the world right
there's lots of different places that

00:25:35.669 --> 00:25:39.480
you can act in the world and there's
lots of different ways you can look at

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:44.190
it and survive that's why you can be a
plumber and a lawyer and an engineer and

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:49.020
those all work right even though they're
very different modes of being and you

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:54.110
can have different personalities and
survive as long as you're capable of

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:59.640
finding the place where your particular
filters and behavioral proclivities

00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:04.230
match the demand of the environment and
a huge part I would say of successful

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:09.679
adaptation is precisely that now there
are other elements of personality too

00:26:09.679 --> 00:26:14.370
one of the things that I've been struck
by and this is actually one of the

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:17.970
criticisms I have of the psycho analysts
and the clinicians in general even

00:26:17.970 --> 00:26:21.780
though I have great admiration for them
and would say that what they have to say

00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:27.270
is very much worth listening to is that
it's not obvious that your personality

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:33.270
is inside you you know what you think
you know a human being is a strange

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:37.200
multi-level thing and you might ask
yourself well for example

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:43.530
know is your mother more a part of you
than your arm or maybe even more

00:26:43.530 --> 00:26:48.659
precisely is your child more a part of
you than your arm it's certainly people

00:26:48.659 --> 00:26:53.730
will do drastically self-sacrificing
things to maintain the lives of their

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:58.020
children and so you're you're a person
and you're made out of all sub

00:26:58.020 --> 00:27:02.190
sort of subcomponents of a person none
of which you could see when you look at

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:05.970
a person all the complicated machinery
inside you that makes you who you are

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:10.860
and then outside of that of course
you're nested in all sorts of complex

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:14.670
systems so you're part of a family and
and you're part of a community and

00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:18.330
that's part of a province and that's
part of the state and that's part of an

00:27:18.330 --> 00:27:23.970
international consortium of states and
that's part of an ecosystem and how you

00:27:23.970 --> 00:27:30.120
make a distinction between you and the
systems that you're embedded in is also

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:34.290
of extraordinary difficulty and when it
and one of the things that you have to

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:37.230
do as a clinical psychologist for
example if you're trying to diagnose

00:27:37.230 --> 00:27:41.130
someone with depression you think you
think well this person's dreadfully

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:45.630
unhappy well you can think about that as
a problem with their psychological

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:50.610
adjustment you know the way that they're
looking at the world but if you look at

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:55.800
the epidemiological literature for
example one of the things that you find

00:27:55.800 --> 00:28:01.680
is that very many people have a first
depressive episode after something

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:05.520
genuinely terrible has happened to them
right they've lost someone or they've

00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:09.570
become injured or or they've become
unemployed because unemployment is a

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:14.670
terrible shock to people and it's not
precisely self-evident that you can

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:18.390
consider someone who's unhappy and
desperate because they no longer have a

00:28:18.390 --> 00:28:26.280
job depressed they're certainly sad and
they're not doing very well but the fact

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:30.810
that they no longer have an income is
actually something with dramatic

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:37.100
practical consequences and treating that
as if it's a mental disorder seems to be

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:42.270
counterproductive it's also the case for
example that if you're if someone comes

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:47.550
into you to talk to you and they're very
upset and they may manifest the signs of

00:28:47.550 --> 00:28:52.200
say an anxiety disorder or again
depression or other other clinical

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:57.510
features for that matter you have to do
a careful analysis of their manner in

00:28:57.510 --> 00:29:01.230
which they're embedded in their family
because and this is something that we'll

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:07.620
talk about quite thoroughly when we come
to discussing Freud is that well you

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:10.950
know it's not like everybody's families
are necessarily particularly happy

00:29:10.950 --> 00:29:13.950
places to be
you know I mean human beings are very

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:18.620
dependent we have a very long period of
dependency partly because we're so

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:22.920
cortically hyperdeveloped it takes a
very long time to program us into

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:28.170
something that's vaguely capable of
maneuvering on its own and that produces

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:32.670
of course the very tight familial bonds
that we all that we all desperately

00:29:32.670 --> 00:29:39.120
require because who wants to be alone in
the world but it also it also exposes us

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:45.210
to the probability of becoming entangled
into even multi-generational family

00:29:45.210 --> 00:29:50.850
pathology and it isn't obvious always
uncertainty hasn't been to me when I've

00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:55.800
seen my clients that the fundamental
problem with the client is the client

00:29:55.800 --> 00:30:05.100
sometimes the fundamental problem is is
the family and and perhaps that person

00:30:05.100 --> 00:30:09.150
has been identified as the problem
person it's rather convenient for

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:14.390
everyone who's involved to make that
presupposition it's also the case that

00:30:14.390 --> 00:30:19.980
this is the Freudian idea fundamentally
this is the Oedipal idea that it's very

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:24.360
easy for people to become over dependent
on their parents and and for the parents

00:30:24.360 --> 00:30:30.540
to facilitate that and then for the
primary developmental problem for the

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:38.940
individual in fact to become free of the
interfering elements of the family so

00:30:38.940 --> 00:30:44.250
that they can exist as independent
individuals well and then of course

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:49.110
there are cultural variations in that
that make that proposition complex but

00:30:49.110 --> 00:30:55.320
that's a fundamental tenant say of
Freudian psychology a lot of the

00:30:55.320 --> 00:31:00.930
clinical psychologists all of whom that
we're going to study have a pronounced

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:06.330
Western orientation one of the
fundamental presuppositions is that part

00:31:06.330 --> 00:31:10.740
of the hallmark of positive
psychological development is the

00:31:10.740 --> 00:31:15.720
creation of an individual that's capable
of acting independently and that's I

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:20.190
would say an implicit ideal that lurks
at the bottom of the clinical

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:26.090
presuppositions of all the theorists
that are classic psychologists

00:31:26.090 --> 00:31:35.490
so now it's a very old picture it's
Jonah

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:42.750
emerging from the whale it's a variant
of a myth the myth is the dragon myth I

00:31:42.750 --> 00:31:48.269
suppose the dragon myth is that there's
a dragon that lives under the ground

00:31:48.269 --> 00:31:54.630
that's eternal and now and then it rises
out of the ground to threaten the state

00:31:54.630 --> 00:32:02.549
and someone within the state determines
to go confront the dragon voluntarily

00:32:02.549 --> 00:32:08.610
and does so and then brings back
something of great value sometimes if

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:14.880
the the the hero is generally male
sometimes the thing of great value is a

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:19.950
female that the dragon has kidnapped
that's a st. George story and sometimes

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:26.130
it's gold another treasure like in the
story of The Hobbit and story that you

00:32:26.130 --> 00:32:32.450
all know very well it's a classic hero
story and the hero story is another

00:32:32.450 --> 00:32:37.080
fundamental element of the clinical
theories I would say it's predicated on

00:32:37.080 --> 00:32:44.250
the idea that you learn through
voluntary contact with that that

00:32:44.250 --> 00:32:50.789
frightens or disgusts you and that's a
hallmark of psychoanalytic theory Jung,

00:32:50.789 --> 00:32:57.000
Carl Jung who we'll discuss in detail
said his primary dictum was“In

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:59.279
sterquiliniis invenitur” which I'm sure I'm

00:32:59.279 --> 00:33:05.070
massacring because it's Latin but it
meant ”in filth it will be found” and one

00:33:05.070 --> 00:33:09.120
of the hallmarks of the clinical
theories is that within the confines of

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:14.070
everyone's experience and you can think
about this as experience out in the

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:21.539
world or experience in the unconscious
mind there are dirty little secrets

00:33:21.539 --> 00:33:32.940
let's say and skeletons and dreadful old
fears and remnants of abuse and memories

00:33:32.940 --> 00:33:40.100
of pathological behavior and failures of
courage that you leave you undeveloped

00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:47.509
perhaps out of avoidance and that the
psychoanalytic process is precisely the

00:33:47.509 --> 00:33:54.360
careful encounter with those forgotten
and and repressed elements of the self

00:33:54.360 --> 00:34:01.470
in the hope that a clear encounter will
redeem them unites them with the

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:06.989
remainder of your personality and make
yours make you stronger in consequence

00:34:06.989 --> 00:34:11.490
and I would say that that's just a
variant of the manner in which human

00:34:11.490 --> 00:34:15.859
beings learn and we'll talk about this
more in relationship to Piaget because

00:34:15.859 --> 00:34:22.740
you always learn when you're wrong which
is very annoying now what do you learn

00:34:22.740 --> 00:34:26.129
when you're correct you you're walking
in the world you're operating in the

00:34:26.129 --> 00:34:31.799
world you have a sense of what you want
to have happen you're always looking at

00:34:31.799 --> 00:34:35.730
the world through this sense of what you
want to have happen you're acting so

00:34:35.730 --> 00:34:41.520
that what you want to have happen will
happen and when it happens well then

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:45.629
you're happy because well first of all
you get what you want and that's good

00:34:45.629 --> 00:34:50.549
maybe depending on what you want but
it's also good because if you get what

00:34:50.549 --> 00:34:55.169
you want when you act then it turns out
that your model of how to act is valid

00:34:55.169 --> 00:34:58.260
right the outcome that you get what you
want

00:34:58.260 --> 00:35:03.690
indicates no error on the part of your
model but it's very frequently the case

00:35:03.690 --> 00:35:10.049
that when you act to get what you want
you don't get what you want and then

00:35:10.049 --> 00:35:14.730
that's unpleasant because you don't get
what you want but it's even more

00:35:14.730 --> 00:35:18.990
unpleasant because it brings with it the
hint of a suggestion that the manner in

00:35:18.990 --> 00:35:25.770
which you're construing the world is
incorrect at some indeterminate level so

00:35:25.770 --> 00:35:33.089
for example if you tell a party tell a
joke at a party you presume that people

00:35:33.089 --> 00:35:38.190
will attend and then when they hear the
joke they will laugh and then if you

00:35:38.190 --> 00:35:44.460
tell the joke and it goes flat or even
worse disgusts and offends people then

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:48.660
you're going to be taken aback and
that's partly because you didn't get

00:35:48.660 --> 00:35:53.220
what you want and that's not so good but
it's but it's more because there's

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:57.420
something wrong with the way you
conceptualized the situation and then

00:35:57.420 --> 00:36:01.740
you're faced with a problem and the
problem is the emergence of a domain of

00:36:01.740 --> 00:36:05.190
the unknown it's like well what kind of
mistake did you make

00:36:05.190 --> 00:36:10.400
maybe you're not as funny as you think
you are not that could be a big problem

00:36:10.400 --> 00:36:15.270
maybe you're not around people that who
are the way you think they are maybe

00:36:15.270 --> 00:36:19.369
they don't like you as much as you
thought they liked you I mean the

00:36:19.369 --> 00:36:25.380
potential for various paranoid thoughts
of increasing severity to come welling

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:30.810
up at you in a situation where you make
even a trivial social mistake is quite

00:36:30.810 --> 00:36:36.390
broad and when you make an error of that
sort you have to face it and sort

00:36:36.390 --> 00:36:41.040
through all the possibilities so that
you can find out what it was that you

00:36:41.040 --> 00:36:45.750
did wrong and how to retool it so that
in the future you don't make the same

00:36:45.750 --> 00:36:50.490
mistake and that requires well that
requires in some sense what you might

00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:53.970
describe as a journey into the belly of
the beast the beast being that place

00:36:53.970 --> 00:36:58.320
where things have fallen apart and where
you're overwhelmed with negative emotion

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:03.510
and chaos and confusion and that's a
very old story that's the story of the

00:37:03.510 --> 00:37:07.500
journey to the underworld and the hero
is the person who makes the voluntary

00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:11.820
journey to the underworld to collect
what's been languishing down there and

00:37:11.820 --> 00:37:17.580
that's the basic motif of psychoanalytic
theory I would say it's the basic motif

00:37:17.580 --> 00:37:22.950
in some sense of clinical practice
because one of the things that you do as

00:37:22.950 --> 00:37:29.250
a clinician is find out what people are
afraid of and what they're avoiding and

00:37:29.250 --> 00:37:34.380
that can be in their past or in their
present or in their future break it down

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:40.380
into smaller pieces and help them devise
strategies of approach and mastery and

00:37:40.380 --> 00:37:48.180
that improves the quality of their
personality and helps develop them into

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:55.520
people who won't make the same mistakes
over and over again all right so well

00:37:55.520 --> 00:38:04.349
why these this plethora of tools well I
said in some sense being a personality

00:38:04.349 --> 00:38:09.350
psychologist is like being an engineer
you're trying to build better people

00:38:09.350 --> 00:38:15.990
when you might say that if you're a
carpenter or a mechanic that your

00:38:15.990 --> 00:38:21.660
ability to fix a vehicle or build a
house is dependent on your proficiency

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:26.850
with regards to the use of a multitude
of tools and so then you might say well

00:38:26.850 --> 00:38:31.830
the more tools you have at your disposal
the more likely it is that you're going

00:38:31.830 --> 00:38:37.080
to manage things properly and so what I
would like to offer you is the

00:38:37.080 --> 00:38:43.070
possibility that what you're going to
encounter in this course is a series of

00:38:43.070 --> 00:38:49.100
sophisticated conceptual tools that will
help you understand yourself better and

00:38:49.100 --> 00:38:54.480
therefore better orient yourself in the
world I regard this course as intensely

00:38:54.480 --> 00:39:00.540
practical and that's because I believe
that you have nothing to rely on in your

00:39:00.540 --> 00:39:08.040
life that's more crucial to your success
as you move through life than your

00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:12.890
character and your personality that's
what you bring to every situation and

00:39:12.890 --> 00:39:18.900
the more sophisticated you are in
relationship to yourself and others the

00:39:18.900 --> 00:39:23.730
more you understand people the deeper
you understand the nature of your own

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:29.340
being the more likely it is that you're
going to proceed through your life in a

00:39:29.340 --> 00:39:36.440
manner that will make you pleased to
exist rather than displeased to exist

00:39:36.440 --> 00:39:43.140
I've collected the writings of people
that I regard as of incomparable

00:39:43.140 --> 00:39:48.900
brilliance they're difficult to
understand their concepts are complex

00:39:48.900 --> 00:39:54.270
but it's not surprising because the
subject matter is complex and vital and

00:39:54.270 --> 00:39:59.790
so it requires work and I would say try
to keep up on the readings if you would

00:39:59.790 --> 00:40:04.500
it's going to make the course much more
much richer for you and I would say

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:08.430
because people often ask me well how
should I read for this course because

00:40:08.430 --> 00:40:15.120
there's a lot of reading and the answer
is read as if it matters that's the

00:40:15.120 --> 00:40:18.060
right answer
don't be thinking about how it's going

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:20.910
to be tested if you do the readings and
you come to the lectures then

00:40:20.910 --> 00:40:27.720
the tests aren't particularly difficult
but you should read the readings as if

00:40:27.720 --> 00:40:32.069
the person is writing about you and you
should try to understand what the person

00:40:32.069 --> 00:40:37.440
says because it's another tool for you
to use and I would say well with my

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:41.759
clients you know I used the approaches
of one theorist for one client and the

00:40:41.759 --> 00:40:45.089
approaches of another theorist for
another client it seems to me to depend

00:40:45.089 --> 00:40:49.079
